          ],
          "content": "64p^{2}+\\frac{32}{3}pq+\\frac{4}{9}q^{2}\\tag{*(v}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.192,
            0.4,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "3a^{2}+4ab+\\frac{4}{3}b^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.245,
            0.392,
            0.285
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{9}{5}s^{2}+6sv+5v^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.293,
            0.885,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": "(**Hint:** 2 was taken out as a common factor in Example 7. Is it possible to do something similar in Exercises (v) and (vi) above?)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.341,
            0.788,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "2. Find the values of the following using the identity $(a - b)^{2} = a^{2} - 2ab + b^{2}.$ (i) $( 7 9 ) ^ { 2 }$ (ii) (193)<sup>2</sup> (iii) (299)<sup>2</sup>"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.44,
            0.402,
            0.462
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.4. More Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.474,
            0.883,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": "What will happen if we want to find the square of the sum of three numbers $a ,$ b and $c ,$ that is, $(a + b + c)^{2}?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.522,
            0.418,
            0.542
          ],
          "content": "Let us replace $b + c$ by d."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.549,
            0.587,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "We already know, $(a + d)^{2} = a^{2} + 2ad + d^{2}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.579,
            0.635,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": "Thus, replacing d by $( b \\quad + \\quad \\bar { c } )$ , we get $a^{2}+2ad+d^{2}=a^{2}+2a(b+c)+(b+c)^{2}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.626,
            0.609,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{So},(a+b+c)^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+2ac+b^{2}+2bc+c^{2}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.658,
            0.521,
            0.88,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.655,
            0.634,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "It may be more convenient to remember this as."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.701,
            0.587,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": "(a+b+c)^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+2ab+2bc+2ca.",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.729,
            0.634,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": "Let us interpret this geometrically by drawing a square of side $a + b + c$ as shown in Fig. 4.4."
        },
        {
